## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical use of AWS-native tooling to monitor SAP workloads, focusing on a low-friction, maintainable health check at a beginner level, with clear failure criteria and automated reporting.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven health checks with EventBridge and Lambda
- AWS Systems Manager Run Command for remote execution
- SAP health probes (sapcontrol, HANA via SQL)
- S3 JSON output, SNS alerts, IAM least-privilege
- Basic OS metrics collection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Lambda scaffold to trigger Run Command and write to S3
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ssm = new AWS.SSM();
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
const sns = new AWS.SNS();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const instanceId = process.env.INSTANCE_ID;
  const bucketName = process.env.S3_BUCKET;
  const topicArn = process.env.SNS_TOPIC_ARN;

  // Execute health check script via SSM Run Command
  const command = await ssm.sendCommand({
    InstanceIds: [instanceId],
    DocumentName: 'AWS-RunShellScript',
    Parameters: {
      commands: ['./sap-health-check.sh']
    }
  }).promise();

  // Process results and handle alerts
  // ... implementation details
};
```

